{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's answer to 'what single mechanism best explains why that night-time gap is larger in some cities than in others' conflates two distinct physical drivers — radiative geometry (sky view factor / street canyon blocking of longwave cooling) and city size/built density — without establishing which is the single dominant cross-city modulator. By framing the mechanism as 'larger, denser street canyons trap longwave radiation and block sky view' while simultaneously claiming the gap 'scales with city size and built density,' it leaves ambiguous whether the cross-city variation is controlled by geometric radiation trapping or by aggregate urban scale, so it fails to identify a single mechanism as required.","premise":"The problem asks for the single mechanism that best explains cross-city variation in the nocturnal UHI gap. SRC_001 names sky-view/longwave trapping as the physical mechanism but then ties the cross-city modulation to 'city size and built density,' a different (aggregate/structural) variable, and never states which single mechanism dominates.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","quote":"The nocturnal UHI gap scales with city size and built density: larger, denser street canyons trap longwave radiation and block sky view, causing a greater and more pronounced night-time temperature difference compared to smaller, less dense cities."},{"block":"74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","quote":"In larger cities, the increased built density and building height decrease the sky view factor, trapping sensible heat and anthropogenic emissions, which results in a larger nocturnal UHI that scales with city size."}],"target":"56ee991bf937b9678855d620f968dd37f56316bc9f0a6afb724105830f34ad78"}]}